7. DOUBLE HASHING



7.1 Algorithm

1. Calculate the first hash value using "k % 11".


2. Calculate the second hash value using "7 - (k % 7)".


3. Check the position obtained from the first hash function.


4. If collision occurs, use the second hash function to calculate the next position.


5. Continue until an empty position is found.


6. Use the same probing sequence for searching.




---

7.2 C Program – Double Hashing

#include <stdio.h>

#define SIZE 11

void doubleHashing(int a[], int n)
{
int table[SIZE];
int i, key, h1, h2, pos, j;

for (i = 0; i < SIZE; i++)  
    table[i] = -1;  

for (i = 0; i < n; i++)  
{  
    key = a[i];  

    h1 = key % SIZE;  
    h2 = 7 - (key % 7);  

    j = 0;  

    while (1)  
    {  
        pos = (h1 + j * h2) % SIZE;  

        if (table[pos] == -1)  
        {  
            table[pos] = key;  
            break;  
        }  

        j++;  
    }  
}  

printf("\nDouble Hashing:\n");  

for (i = 0; i < SIZE; i++)  
    printf("Index %d : %d\n", i, table[i]);

}

int main()
{
int a[] = {23, 43, 13, 33, 53, 63, 73};

doubleHashing(a, 7);  

return 0;

}


