17. CONCLUSION



The three collision resolution techniques—Linear Probing, Quadratic Probing and Double Hashing—were implemented successfully using the given railway booking IDs.

For the selected hash table size of 11, the given booking IDs produce different initial hash positions. Therefore, no collision occurs during insertion and all three techniques produce the same final hash table.

During searching, different probing sequences are used. For the non-existing key 99, the number of probes differs between the three methods.

The experiment demonstrates that the performance of a hashing technique depends on factors such as the hash function, table size, load factor and number of collisions. The implemented programs can also handle collisions when different input values produce the same hash position.
